# CHAPTER 6 · MACROS

A macro is keystrokes recorded into a register and replayed. Anything
repetitive with structure — reformat 200 lines, wrap every value,
convert a list to code — is one good macro away from done.

## 6.1 RECORD, PLAY, REPEAT

`q{reg}`{normal} starts recording into a register, `q`{normal} stops.
`@{reg}`{normal} plays it. `@@`{normal} replays the last one.
`25@q`{normal} plays it 25 times.

THE GOLDEN RULES of a robust macro:
1. Start with an absolute position: `0`{normal} (line start) or a
   search — never "three words in".
2. Use motions that FAIL when the pattern is absent (`f`{normal},
   `/`{normal}): a failed motion stops the macro, so running it 100
   times on 80 valid lines is safe.
3. End by moving to the next target line (`j`{normal} or
   `n`{normal}) so replays chain.

EXERCISE 1: Turn the three plain lines below into checklist items,
so each reads like:  - [ ] Buy milk
Record on the first line: `qq`{normal} `0`{normal} `i`{normal}- [ ]
(with a trailing space) `<Esc>`{normal} `w`{normal} `~`{normal}
`j`{normal} `q`{normal}. Then play `@q`{normal} for the second line
and `@@`{normal} for the third:

buy milk
clean desk
fix flaky test

## 6.2 APPLY A MACRO WITH :g OR OVER A RANGE

Replaying with counts is fine; replaying by PATTERN is better:

    :g/^err_/normal @w     run macro w on every line starting err_
    :'<,'>normal @w        run it on every line of the selection

EXERCISE 2: Record `qw`{normal} `gUU`{normal} `q`{normal} on the
first err line below, then run `:g/^err_/normal @w`{vim} to
uppercase all three at once:

err_timeout
err_overflow
err_crc_mismatch

## 6.3 EDIT A MACRO LIKE TEXT

A macro IS a register, so you can dump it, fix it, and load it back:

    "qp        paste the macro's keystrokes as text
    (edit the text like any other text)
    "qyy       yank the fixed line back into the register

No re-recording a 20-step macro because of one typo. Special keys
show up as <80>kb-style codes — leave those alone, edit around them.

## 6.4 RECURSIVE MACROS (KNOW IT EXISTS)

A macro that ends with `@q`{normal} calling itself runs until a
motion fails — `qqqqq`{normal}...record body...`@q`{normal}
`q`{normal}. Powerful, but `:g/.../normal @q`{vim} covers the same
ground with more control. Reach for recursion when line count is
unknown and the stop condition is "motion fails".

## WORKOUT

Real-world rep: take any CSV-ish or list-ish text in a scratch
buffer and convert it to code with ONE macro: anchor with
`0`{normal}, edit, hop with `f`{normal}/`/`{normal}, end on
`j`{normal}, then `@@`{normal} or `:g//normal @q`{vim} the rest. If
the macro breaks halfway, dump it with "qp, repair, reload.

